Chapter: RecordFieldSilence

 A record literal names every field of its type, and no other.

 Both halves of this were silent until 2026-07-14, and both were the
 same mistake wearing different clothes: the emitter writes the fields
 it was given and leaves the rest as __alloc left them, which is zero.

 A field left OUT came back as zero, and zero is a plausible value for
 an integer, an empty list and a null pointer alike, so nothing
 downstream could tell it was never set. That cost a real
 memory-corruption bug -- net-io-send-chunk rebuilt a TcpTransportState
 naming four of its seven fields, so every send zeroed a raw heap
 address and the next inbound frame wrote bytes off the wire to
 physical address 0, over the IDT.

 A field MISSPELLED was dropped on the floor and the field the author
 meant to set kept its zero. CDX2005 already existed for exactly this
 mistake on a field access; the literal was the one place it was never
 raised.

 missing-one is CDX2006. wrong-name is CDX2005, and it is also CDX2006,
 because naming a field that does not exist necessarily leaves a real
 one unnamed.

Section: Definitions

  Point = record {
   x : Integer,
   y : Integer
  }

  missing-one : Point
  missing-one = Point { x = 1 }

  wrong-name : Point
  wrong-name = Point { x = 1, z = 2 }

  opening : Integer = 0
